module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Sites.Etale
public import WeilConjectures.Mathlib.RingTheory.Henselian
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.EtaleAcyclic
import Mathlib.Algebra.Category.Ring.FinitePresentation
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Flat.FaithfullyFlat.Algebra
import WeilConjectures.AlgebraicGeometry.Sites.StrictlyHenselian
import WeilConjectures.Topology.SpectralSpace.ConnectedComponent
import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.ProEtale.RetractionsStrictlyHenselian

/-!
# Affine étale covers and strictly henselian component rings

Source: Hübner–Schmidt, *The tame site of a scheme*, §6 component-ring convention (printed
p. 12), Definition 7.8 (printed p. 14) and Proposition 7.9, étale case (printed p. 15)
(blueprint: lem-trf-affine-etale-cover-component-criterion).

The component ideal is generated by idempotents vanishing on a connected component.
The component spectrum map records the canonical closed subscheme.  Splitting of all
jointly surjective étale families over `Spec C` is the landed predicate
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IsEtaleAcyclic` (def-trf-etale-acyclic-scheme).

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.componentIdeal`: the ideal `I_Z` of a connected component `Z`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.componentRing`: the component ring `C_Z = C / I_Z`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.componentSpectrumMap`: the map `Spec C_Z → Spec C`.

## Main statements

* `AlgebraicGeometry.affineEtaleCover_componentCriterion`.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits
open AlgebraicGeometry

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The ideal generated by idempotents vanishing on the connected component of `x`.

Source: Hübner–Schmidt, *The tame site of a scheme*, §6 component-ring convention
(blueprint: lem-trf-affine-etale-cover-component-criterion).
-/
noncomputable def componentIdeal (C : Type u) [CommRing C] (x : PrimeSpectrum C) : Ideal C :=
  Ideal.span {e : C | IsIdempotentElem e ∧ ∀ p ∈ connectedComponent x, e ∈ p.asIdeal}

/-- The component ring associated with a point of an affine spectrum.

Source: Hübner–Schmidt, *The tame site of a scheme*, §6 component-ring convention
(blueprint: lem-trf-affine-etale-cover-component-criterion).
-/
noncomputable def componentRing (C : Type u) [CommRing C] (x : PrimeSpectrum C) : Type u :=
  C ⧸ componentIdeal C x

/-- The component ring carries the commutative ring structure of the quotient `C / I_Z`.

Source: Hübner–Schmidt, *The tame site of a scheme*, §6 component-ring convention
(blueprint: lem-trf-affine-etale-cover-component-criterion).
-/
noncomputable instance componentRing.instCommRing (C : Type u) [CommRing C]
    (x : PrimeSpectrum C) : CommRing (componentRing C x) :=
  Ideal.Quotient.commRing (componentIdeal C x)

/-- The canonical map from the spectrum of a component ring to the spectrum of `C`.

Source: Hübner–Schmidt, *The tame site of a scheme*, §6 component-ring convention
(blueprint: lem-trf-affine-etale-cover-component-criterion).
-/
noncomputable def componentSpectrumMap (C : Type u) [CommRing C] (x : PrimeSpectrum C) :
    PrimeSpectrum (C ⧸ componentIdeal C x) → PrimeSpectrum C :=
  PrimeSpectrum.comap (Ideal.Quotient.mk (componentIdeal C x))

/-- The component quotient has the component as its underlying closed subscheme, and
splitting all jointly surjective étale families over `Spec C` is equivalent to strict
henselianity of every component ring.

Source: Hübner–Schmidt, *The tame site of a scheme*, §6 component-ring convention,
Definition 7.8 and Proposition 7.9, étale case
(blueprint: lem-trf-affine-etale-cover-component-criterion).

Proof (agent-written; Stacks Algebra 00EG, 00EE, 00E8, 00QO, 00U2, 00HQ, Topology 005F,
Pro-étale 097V):
1. Put `X = Spec C`.  The `D(f)` form a basis, `D(f) ≅ Spec C_f`
   (lem-weil-pbc-spec-localization-away-basic-open), each such spectrum and `X` are
   quasi-compact (lem-weil-pbc-spec-basic-opens-unit-ideal-quasi-compact), and
   `D(f) ∩ D(g) = D(fg)`; so `X` satisfies the hypotheses of the component-intersection
   theorem.
2. By lem-weil-pbc-quasicompact-components-clopen-intersection a component `Z` is the
   intersection of its clopen neighbourhoods, which are the `D(e)` for idempotents `e`
   with `Z ⊆ D(e)` (lem-weil-pbc-spec-clopen-idempotent).  Hence `⋂ D(e) = V(I_Z)`, which
   gives the first conjunct.  Moreover `C_Z = colim_e C_e` with `C_e = C/(1-e)`: the
   directed union of the kernels `(1-e)` is `I_Z`, since for idempotents `d_i` vanishing
   on `Z` the idempotent `e = ∏ (1 - d_i)` is `1` on `Z` and kills every `d_i`.
3. Assume every `C_Z` strictly henselian.  Refine a covering family by affine étale charts
   `Spec B → X` (lem-weil-pbc-etale-composition-cancellation).  Over a component `Z` choose a
   point of a chart above the closed point of `Spec C_Z`; the base change to `C_Z` is étale
   (lem-weil-pbc-etale-ring-base-change) and has a section over `Spec C_Z`
   (lem-sbc-strict-henselian-etale-neighbourhood-section), i.e. a `C`-algebra map
   `B → C_Z` (def-weil-affine-spectrum).  As `B` is finitely presented
   (lem-weil-pbc-etale-algebra-finite-presentation-localization) it factors through some
   `C_e` with `Z ⊆ D(e)` (lem-weil-pbc-finite-presentation-algebra-factors-filtered-colimit),
   a section over the open `D(e) = Spec C_e` (lem-weil-pbc-principal-localization-open-immersion).
4. Finitely many such `D(e)` cover `X` (lem-weil-pbc-spec-basic-opens-unit-ideal-quasi-compact);
   make them disjoint clopens `P_j = W_j \ ⋃_{i<j} W_i` and glue the sections into the
   coproduct.  If `C = 0` the unique map from the empty scheme is a section.
5. Conversely assume every étale cover splits.  For `f̄ ∈ C_Z` with lift `f`, the cover
   `Spec C_f, Spec C_{1-f} → X` is étale and surjective
   (lem-weil-pbc-etale-algebra-finite-presentation-localization,
   lem-weil-pbc-spec-localization-away-basic-open); a section forces `Z ⊆ D(f)` or
   `Z ⊆ D(1-f)`, so `f̄` or `1 - f̄` is a unit
   (lem-weil-pbc-spec-basic-opens-unit-ideal-quasi-compact); hence `C_Z` is local.
6. A faithfully flat étale `C`-algebra `B` gives a surjective singleton cover
   (lem-weil-pbc-faithfully-flat-surjective-spectrum), whose section is a retraction
   `B → C` (def-weil-affine-spectrum); so every `C_𝔪` is strictly henselian
   (lem-weil-pbc-split-etale-covers-maximal-local-criterion).
7. With `𝔪` the inverse image of the maximal ideal of `C_Z`, the quotient map induces
   `C_𝔪 ≅ C_Z` (def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime), so `C_Z` is strictly henselian.
-/
theorem affineEtaleCover_componentCriterion (C : Type u) [CommRing C] :
    (∀ x : PrimeSpectrum C,
      Set.range (componentSpectrumMap C x) = connectedComponent x) ∧
    ((∀ x : PrimeSpectrum C, IsStrictlyHenselianLocalRing (componentRing C x)) ↔
      Scheme.IsEtaleAcyclic (Spec (.of C))) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
